D={
"Battle of Hastings":"Edward the Confessor died childless in January 1066, and three men claimed England's throne. Harold Godwinson was crowned, defeated the Norwegian king Harald Hardrada at Stamford Bridge, then marched south to meet William of Normandy, who had landed at Pevensey. On 14 October 1066, after a day-long fight, Harold was killed (perhaps by an arrow in the eye, as the Bayeux Tapestry suggests). William was crowned on Christmas Day, built castles, replaced the English nobility with Normans and ordered the Domesday Book; French words flooded into English.",
"First Crusade":"In 1095, after the Byzantine emperor asked for help against the Seljuk Turks, Pope Urban II called on Western Christians to free Jerusalem, promising remission of sins. A popular 'People's Crusade' massacred Jews in the Rhineland before being destroyed in Anatolia. The knights' armies took Antioch (1098) and Jerusalem (July 1099), slaughtering many Muslim and Jewish inhabitants, and founded four crusader states. Muslim memory of this violence shaped later relations, and Saladin retook Jerusalem in 1187.",
"Second Battle of Tarain":"In 1191 the Chauhan king Prithviraj III defeated Muhammad of Ghor at Tarain near Thanesar. Ghori returned in 1192 with mounted archers who used feigned retreats to break the Rajput army; Prithviraj was captured and killed. Ghori's slave-general Qutb ud-Din Aibak took Delhi, and after Ghori's death founded the Delhi Sultanate in 1206, beginning six centuries of Turkic, Afghan and Mughal rule in north India.",
"Mongol conquests":"After Temüjin was proclaimed Genghis Khan in 1206, Mongol armies of mounted archers, organised in decimal units with strict discipline and fast messengers, conquered northern China, destroyed the Khwarazmian Empire (1219–21), and, under his successors, Russia (1237–40), Baghdad (1258) and all of China (1279). Cities that resisted were often massacred; tens of millions may have died. Yet the Pax Mongolica also reopened the Silk Road, allowing Marco Polo's travels and the spread of gunpowder, paper and, tragically, the Black Death. They were stopped by the Mamluks at Ain Jalut (1260), the Delhi Sultanate, and storms off Japan.",
"Destruction of Nalanda":"Nalanda, a great monastic university founded in the 5th century, had trained scholars from China, Tibet and Korea for 700 years. Around 1193 forces of Bakhtiyar Khalji sacked Nalanda and Vikramashila, killing monks; the Persian historian Minhaj-i-Siraj records the attacks, and later tradition says the library burned for months. Combined with the decline of royal patronage, it ended institutional Buddhism in India, though it survived in Tibet, Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia. A new Nalanda University opened nearby in 2014.",
"Magna Carta":"King John of England lost Normandy to France, raised heavy taxes and quarrelled with the Pope. In June 1215 rebel barons forced him to seal the 'Great Charter' at Runnymede, which said that no free man could be imprisoned or punished except by lawful judgement of his peers or the law of the land, and that the king must consult barons on taxes. The Pope annulled it within weeks and civil war followed, but reissued versions became part of English law. Later, Parliament, American colonists and Indian freedom fighters cited it as a symbol of rights against arbitrary power.",
"Sack of Baghdad":"Hulagu Khan's Mongol army, with Christian Georgian and Armenian allies and Chinese siege engineers, besieged Baghdad in January–February 1258. After the caliph al-Musta'sim surrendered, the city was sacked for a week; estimates of the dead range from tens of thousands to hundreds of thousands, and the caliph was reportedly rolled in a carpet and trampled. The House of Wisdom's books were said to have turned the Tigris black with ink. The fall ended the Abbasid caliphate in Baghdad and shifted Islamic cultural centres to Cairo, Delhi and later Istanbul.",
"Fall of Constantinople":"By 1453 the Byzantine Empire was little more than its capital, defended by about 7,000 men. Sultan Mehmed II, 21 years old, besieged it with perhaps 80,000 troops and giant bronze cannon cast by the Hungarian engineer Orban, and dragged ships overland into the Golden Horn. On 29 May the walls were breached; the last emperor, Constantine XI, died fighting. Mehmed made the city his capital, turned Hagia Sophia into a mosque, and Greek scholars who fled to Italy helped fuel the Renaissance; Europeans sought sea routes to Asia, leading to the Age of Exploration.",
"Gutenberg printing press":"Around 1440–1450 in Mainz, Johannes Gutenberg combined movable metal type cast in a hand mould, oil-based ink and a screw press adapted from wine presses. His Bible (about 1455) was printed in some 180 copies. By 1500 presses in over 250 European cities had printed perhaps 20 million volumes. Printing spread Renaissance learning, made Luther's Reformation possible and standardised languages. Earlier, China had invented woodblock printing (Diamond Sutra, 868) and Korea metal type (Jikji, 1377).",
"Columbus reaches the Americas":"Christopher Columbus, a Genoese navigator sponsored by Ferdinand and Isabella of Spain, sailed west seeking Asia and landed in the Bahamas on 12 October 1492, then reached Cuba and Hispaniola. He believed he had reached the Indies, hence the name 'Indians'. His voyages began European colonisation, the Columbian Exchange of crops and animals, the enslavement and catastrophic population collapse of the Taíno and other peoples, and later the Atlantic slave trade.",
"Vasco da Gama reaches India":"Sailing around the Cape of Good Hope with an East African pilot's help, Vasco da Gama reached Calicut (Kozhikode) on 20 May 1498. The Zamorin was unimpressed by his gifts, but he returned to Lisbon with spices worth many times the voyage's cost. Portugal soon used warships and forts (Goa, 1510) to control Indian Ocean trade, beginning European colonial involvement in India.",
"First Battle of Panipat":"On 21 April 1526 Babur, ruler of Kabul, with about 12,000–25,000 men, met Sultan Ibrahim Lodi's much larger army and about 100 elephants. Babur used the tulughma flanking tactic and field guns and matchlocks behind carts; the guns panicked the elephants. Ibrahim Lodi died in battle, the first Indian ruler killed on the battlefield in this era, and Babur founded the Mughal Empire.",
"Fall of Tenochtitlan":"Hernán Cortés landed in Mexico in 1519 with about 500 men and allied with the Tlaxcalans and others who resented Aztec tribute. Moctezuma II received him, was taken hostage and died in 1520; the Spanish were driven out on the 'Noche Triste'. A smallpox epidemic then killed many, including Emperor Cuitláhuac. After a 75-day siege with brigantines on the lake, Tenochtitlan fell on 13 August 1521; Mexico City was built on its ruins.",
"Spanish conquest of the Inca":"Francisco Pizarro arrived in 1532 as the Inca Empire was weakened by smallpox and a civil war between Atahualpa and Huáscar. At Cajamarca, Pizarro's 168 men ambushed Atahualpa's unarmed retinue, captured him, collected a room full of gold as ransom, and executed him in 1533. Resistance continued until the last Inca, Túpac Amaru, was executed in 1572.",
"Battle of Talikota":"On 23 January 1565 an alliance of the Deccan sultanates of Bijapur, Ahmadnagar, Golconda and Bidar defeated Vijayanagara's army under the regent Aliya Rama Raya, who was captured and beheaded. Two Muslim commanders in Vijayanagara service reportedly switched sides. The capital Hampi was looted for six months and never recovered; its ruins are now a UNESCO site.",
"Founding of the East India Company":"On 31 December 1600 Elizabeth I chartered the 'Governor and Company of Merchants of London trading into the East Indies'. It set up factories at Surat (1613), Madras (1639), Bombay (1668) and Calcutta (1690), trading in cotton, silk, indigo, saltpetre and tea. After Plassey (1757) it became a ruler with a private army of 260,000, until the Crown took over in 1858.",
"Atlantic slave trade":"From the 16th to 19th centuries about 12.5 million Africans were shipped across the Atlantic, and nearly 2 million died on the Middle Passage. Enslaved people worked on sugar, tobacco and cotton plantations in Brazil, the Caribbean and North America. African resistance, revolts like Haiti's, and abolitionist movements led Britain to ban the trade in 1807 and slavery in 1833; Brazil was last in 1888.",
"Thirty Years' War":"Beginning in 1618 when Protestant nobles threw imperial officials out of a window in Prague, the war pitted Catholic Habsburgs against Protestant princes, Sweden and Catholic France. Armies, famine and plague killed perhaps 8 million, a third of some German regions. The Peace of Westphalia (1648) recognised state sovereignty.",
"Battle of Plassey":"On 23 June 1757 Robert Clive's 3,000 troops faced Nawab Siraj ud-Daulah's 50,000. Clive had bribed the Nawab's commander Mir Jafar, who held back his troops. The Nawab fled and was killed; Mir Jafar became a puppet Nawab, and the Company gained control of Bengal's revenues.",
"Third Battle of Panipat":"On 14 January 1761 the Maratha army under Sadashivrao Bhau fought Ahmad Shah Durrani's Afghan army and Indian allies. Short of food, the Marathas were crushed; perhaps 100,000 died. The defeat checked Maratha expansion and helped the British rise.",
"Battle of Buxar":"On 22 October 1764 the Company defeated the combined forces of Mir Qasim, the Nawab of Awadh and the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II. The 1765 Treaty of Allahabad gave the Company the diwani, the right to collect revenue in Bengal, Bihar and Odisha.",
"Great Bengal Famine":"In 1769–70 drought and Company revenue extraction caused a famine that killed perhaps 10 million in Bengal, about a third of the population. The Company maintained, even increased, tax collections.",
"American Revolution":"Colonists protested British taxes ('no taxation without representation'), fought from 1775, declared independence on 4 July 1776, and won with French help at Yorktown (1781). The 1787 Constitution created a federal republic, though slavery remained.",
"French Revolution":"Bankruptcy, hunger and Enlightenment ideas led to the storming of the Bastille (14 July 1789), the Declaration of the Rights of Man, the execution of Louis XVI (1793) and the Terror, then Napoleon's rise.",
"Haitian Revolution":"In 1791 enslaved Africans in Saint-Domingue rose up; Toussaint Louverture led them, and Haiti became independent in 1804, the first nation founded by formerly enslaved people.",
"Napoleonic Wars":"From 1803 to 1815 Napoleon fought coalitions across Europe, winning at Austerlitz but losing in Russia (1812) and at Waterloo (1815). His Code spread legal equality.",
"Indian Rebellion of 1857":"Sepoys at Meerut rose on 10 May 1857 over greased cartridges, annexations and grievances. Delhi, Kanpur, Lucknow and Jhansi became centres, with Bahadur Shah Zafar, Rani Lakshmibai and Tatya Tope. It was crushed with great brutality; the Company was abolished in 1858.",
"Opium Wars":"Britain fought China (1839–42, 1856–60) to force opium imports from India; China ceded Hong Kong and opened ports — the 'century of humiliation'.",
"Abolition of slavery in the British Empire":"The Slavery Abolition Act of 1833 freed about 800,000 enslaved people; slave owners, not the enslaved, received compensation of £20 million.",
"American Civil War":"From 1861 to 1865 the Union defeated the slave-holding Confederacy; about 620,000 soldiers died. Lincoln's Emancipation Proclamation and the 13th Amendment ended slavery.",
"Scramble for Africa":"Between 1881 and 1914 European powers colonised about 90% of Africa, drawing borders at the Berlin Conference and exploiting resources and labour.",
"Great Famine of 1876–78":"Drought struck south India while grain was exported and relief was minimal; about 5.5–10 million died.",
"Founding of the Indian National Congress":"Founded in Bombay in December 1885 with A. O. Hume, Dadabhai Naoroji and others, it became the main vehicle of the freedom movement.",
"Partition of Bengal":"Lord Curzon split Bengal in 1905 along religious lines; the Swadeshi movement boycotted British goods, and the partition was reversed in 1911.",
"World War I":"After the assassination of Franz Ferdinand (1914), alliances drew Europe into war. Trench warfare, poison gas and tanks killed about 17 million; 1.3 million Indian soldiers served. Empires collapsed.",
"Russian Revolution":"In 1917 war and hunger toppled the tsar (February); Lenin's Bolsheviks seized power in October, leading to civil war and the Soviet Union.",
"Jallianwala Bagh massacre":"On 13 April 1919, General Dyer ordered troops to fire on a peaceful crowd in Amritsar, killing hundreds. It turned many Indians against British rule.",
"Non-Cooperation Movement":"In 1920–22 Gandhi called for boycotts of British courts, schools and goods; it was called off after violence at Chauri Chaura.",
"Great Depression":"The 1929 Wall Street crash led to a global depression; unemployment soared, helping extremists rise.",
"Salt March":"In March–April 1930 Gandhi walked 385 km to Dandi to make salt in defiance of the salt tax; tens of thousands were jailed.",
"World War II":"From 1939 to 1945 Allies fought the Axis; about 70–85 million died, including 6 million Jews in the Holocaust. 2.5 million Indians served.",
"The Holocaust":"Nazi Germany murdered about 6 million Jews and millions of others in camps and mass shootings.",
"Quit India Movement":"In August 1942 Gandhi demanded the British leave; leaders were jailed and protests crushed.",
"Bengal famine of 1943":"About 3 million died in Bengal amid wartime policies, hoarding and the loss of Burmese rice.",
"Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki":"US atomic bombs killed about 110,000–210,000 people in August 1945; Japan surrendered.",
"Indian Independence and Partition":"On 15 August 1947 India and Pakistan became independent; partition displaced 15 million and killed up to 1–2 million.",
"Indian Constitution adopted":"The Constitution came into force on 26 January 1950, making India a sovereign democratic republic.",
}
